<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Zhao</h1><p class="st-meta">319 CE – 351 CE · capital Xiangguo, Ye</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Shi Le, a former slave of Jie origin who conquered most of northern China, and of his notoriously cruel successor Shi Hu.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jie-people">Jie</a> tribesman once sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, became the most successful general of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a>. In 319 he broke away and proclaimed himself King of Zhao at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xingtai">Xiangguo</a> (Xingtai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>). He captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-zhao">Han Zhao</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yao">Liu Yao</a> in 328, destroyed his state in 329 and declared himself emperor in 330.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-le">Shi Le</a>, though illiterate, had the histories read to him, set up schools and recruited Chinese officials while keeping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jie-people">Jie</a> and other tribal peoples as a privileged military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>. The Buddhist monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fotudeng">Fotudeng</a> advised both him and his successor, helping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> spread in the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-hu">Shi Hu</a>, who seized power in 334 and moved the capital to Ye, built lavish palaces and imposed crushing labour and military levies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-hu">Shi Hu's</a> death in 349 his sons fought for the throne. His adopted grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ran-min">Ran Min</a> seized Ye in 350 and ordered the massacre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jie-people">Jie</a>, founding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ran-wei">Ran Wei</a>; the last Later Zhao ruler was killed in 351.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-zhao.jpg" alt="Later Zhao" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_338_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>319 CE – 351 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Xiangguo, Ye</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Shi Le, Shi Hu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zhao (Shi Le)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=335&amp;focus=later-zhao" aria-label="Later Zhao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7559 869 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Zhao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7879 1125L7810 1131M7879 1125L7879 1125"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7810 1131h0M7879 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7879 1125h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7879 1125h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=335&amp;focus=later-zhao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
